## 1. The Local-to-CI Parity Principle

Before creating cloud pipelines, you must enforce the **Local-to-CI Parity Principle**:

```
========================================================================================
THE LOCAL-TO-CI PARITY PRINCIPLE:
Every test, lint rule, and compilation command executed by Bitrise in the cloud 
must be runnable and passing locally on your developer workstation.
Never rely on CI to discover basic syntax errors, type mismatches, or missing imports.
========================================================================================
```

If a command fails on your local machine, it is guaranteed to fail on Bitrise. Standardizing your project scripts ensures that both developers and cloud virtual machines run identical commands.

---

## 2. Step 1: Standardize `package.json` Scripts

Bitrise workflows require predictable command names to run quality checks across any project in the enterprise.

Open your project root <code>package.json</code> and ensure the <code>scripts</code> section contains these four standard hooks:

